CORE
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ copy-initialized empty base: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ constructor template: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ value-initialized empty base: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
An empty base subobject shares its storage with the derived object's
other subobjects.  Its initialization by a mem-initializer was lowered to
an assignment of the class's one-byte representation, which overwrote the
first byte of the pointer stored there.  libc++'s std::bind(&S::f, &s, _1)
stores _1 in such a base, next to the bound &s.
